WEBVTT

NOTE Text: the publisher's Official Report, unchanged. Timing: aligned automatically to the audio by signed-track-manifest.

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:09.500
The Presiding Officer: We now move to
First Minister’s question time for

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:14.100
party leaders. Before we begin, I invite
members to join me in welcoming to the

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:19.300
gallery Her Excellency Sanja
Štiglic, ambassador of the

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:20.720
Republic of Slovenia.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:26.120
[Applause.]

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:42.320
Malcolm Offord: When John Swinney
became First Minister in May 2024,

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:47.000
he promised to lead a delivery-focused,
unifying Government that is focused on

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:51.600
four priorities: eradicating child
poverty, growing the economy,

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:54.820
improving the national health service
and reforming public services.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:01.200
Why did he suddenly divert to Cardiff for
a taxpayer-funded wee pretendy summit?

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:05.580
Why has he given up on delivery
and unity and instead chosen

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:07.500
distraction and division?

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:12.880
Which of those four priorities were
advanced by his wee day trip to Wales?

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:23.400
The First Minister: One of the many
elements of the Government that I have

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.360
concentrated on, and on which I have
given the Government leadership,

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:30.960
is ensuring that we have a
focus on four major themes:

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:36.820
eradicating child poverty, growing the
economy, making the transition to net zero

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:38.720
and improving our public services.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:42.980
We are making significant progress
in advancing that agenda.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:48.900
That might partly explain why
I won the election on 7 May,

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:52.180
which is a rather important
point that I add to the mix.

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:57.280
The people of Scotland heard from me
during the election campaign about the

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:02.320
importance of self-determination for the
people of Scotland and of Scotland being

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:03.660
an independent country.

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:06.553
If we had the full powers of independence,
that option for Scotland—that

00:02:06.553 --> 00:02:12.320
opportunity—would help us to achieve
our objectives of having a stronger

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:14.280
economy and tackling child poverty.

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:18.380
That is why I went to Cardiff on Monday.

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:29.450
Malcolm Offord: Let me explain to John
Swinney why he is delusional to focus on

00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:30.530
that issue at the moment.

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:34.650
The two most recent Westminster and
Holyrood elections were the worst for the

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:36.150
Scottish National Party since 2014.

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:42.030
Under John Swinney’s leadership, support
for the separatist parties is now at an

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:47.730
all-time low—only 1 million votes in
May 2016, which is down from 1.6 million

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:49.550
votes in indyref.

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:54.150
[Interruption.]

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:56.070
The Presiding Officer:
Excuse me, Mr Flynn.

00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:58.310
Malcolm Offord: Mr Flynn is emitting
more gas than a CalMac ferry.

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:08.030
In contrast, 2 million Scots voted
to remain in the United Kingdom,

00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:09.390
and that was for a generation.

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:15.050
This so-called Celtic alliance pact
is nothing but smoke and mirrors,

00:03:15.970 --> 00:03:17.370
distraction and division.

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:22.890
Surely the First Minister must agree that
he needs to focus on the day job and deal

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:25.550
with our faltering economy,
our declining education,

00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:31.450
our struggling NHS, our dysfunctional
transport, our chaotic immigration and our

00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:33.030
rising welfare spending.

00:03:36.710 --> 00:03:39.810
The First Minister: I have set out the
Government’s programme, which covers all

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:43.070
the key areas of domestic policy
and areas of responsibility.

00:03:43.930 --> 00:03:47.570
As I said to Malcolm Offord in my
first answer, the Government is making

00:03:47.570 --> 00:03:52.690
significant progress on all those issues,
which is perhaps one of the reasons why we

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:53.770
won the election.

00:03:53.770 --> 00:03:59.390
I point out to Malcolm Offord that
58 SNP MSPs were elected and only

00:03:59.390 --> 00:04:04.790
17 Reform MSPs, and we won the election
with the biggest gap between us and our

00:04:04.790 --> 00:04:07.190
nearest rival in the
history of devolution.

00:04:07.670 --> 00:04:11.970
I do not think that I need any lectures
from a losing force such as Malcolm

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:13.950
Offord, the leader of Reform.

00:04:14.510 --> 00:04:17.670
As for the question of “a
generation”, I note that,

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:23.010
by the end of the decade, a million
individuals in Scotland will not have been

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:27.390
asked their opinion in a referendum
on the question of independence.

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:31.890
That is one of the reasons why it is so
important that the people of this country

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:36.150
have the chance to decide their own
future and to opt for independence.

00:04:46.070 --> 00:04:48.710
Malcolm Offord: Based on that response, it
is clear that Piddington has more chance

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:50.710
of becoming independent than
John Swinney’s Scotland.

00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:54.090
Why is that? It is because the
SNP has not prepared for it.

00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:58.710
In the 12 years since 2014, the SNP could
have used its devolved powers to make

00:04:58.710 --> 00:05:01.310
Scotland the most
successful part of the UK.

00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:04.850
It could have spent 12 years answering
the big four questions in relation to

00:05:04.850 --> 00:05:08.310
separation: currency,
pensions, defence and borders.

00:05:08.890 --> 00:05:10.555
Instead, what do we have?

00:05:10.555 --> 00:05:15.550
We have an economy with no growth; our
world-famous education has been trashed;

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:19.090
NHS Scotland is on its knees; our
roads and ferries are in chaos;

00:05:19.690 --> 00:05:21.830
uncontrolled immigration is
upsetting our communities;

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:26.510
the police are starved of cash; local
councils are about to be butchered;

00:05:26.510 --> 00:05:30.370
welfare is at an all-time high;
taxation is at an all-time high;

00:05:30.850 --> 00:05:34.650
and we have a black hole of £5
billion, despite a record block grant

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:36.130
of £26 billion. That

00:05:37.910 --> 00:05:41.190
is chronic mismanagement of the public
finances—and I have not even quoted the

00:05:41.190 --> 00:05:44.463
Auditor General of Scotland on
the missing ScotWind moneys.

00:05:44.463 --> 00:05:48.690
All of that shame list
relates to devolved matters;

00:05:49.610 --> 00:05:54.030
John Swinney cannot blame Westminster for
any of that omnishambles—it is entirely

00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:56.970
home grown. This debacle
was created by the SNP.

00:05:57.470 --> 00:06:01.150
How can John Swinney blame Westminster
and say that Westminster is broken when

00:06:01.150 --> 00:06:02.830
Holyrood is in such a mess?

00:06:04.870 --> 00:06:10.610
Is the real reason that John
Swinney went to Cardiff

00:06:10.610 --> 00:06:14.150
that he would rather grandstand to the
media than face the uncomfortable truth

00:06:14.150 --> 00:06:16.230
that he is failing in his four priorities?

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:20.450
Scotland does not want a
referendum any time soon—

00:06:20.850 --> 00:06:21.970
[Interruption.]

00:06:21.970 --> 00:06:23.950
—so will the

00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:32.290
First Minister please honour Scotland’s
wish to leave this for a generation?

00:06:33.610 --> 00:06:39.320
The First Minister: It is really
quite remarkable that Malcolm

00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:41.220
Offord—who seems to think that
he, having lost the election,

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:44.440
knows the views of the people
of Scotland better than

00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:52.200
I, who in fact won the election,
do—seems to be in a very agitated state

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:54.580
about the prospect of
Scottish independence.

00:06:55.220 --> 00:07:00.120
It suggests that he knows that he has
something to fear at the prospect of being

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:05.140
able to take our own decisions on the
economy, to use the energy wealth of

00:07:05.140 --> 00:07:10.020
Scotland to lower the bills for the people
of our country and to make sure that our

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:15.460
trading relationships with Europe, which
Malcolm Offord and his ilk absolutely

00:07:15.460 --> 00:07:16.720
sabotaged, are good.

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:21.020
That is perhaps an illustration
of why the people of Scotland,

00:07:21.020 --> 00:07:24.700
in increasing numbers, as
demonstrated by recent opinion polls,

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:28.760
support the concept of Scottish
independence and why people in Scotland

00:07:28.760 --> 00:07:32.600
want that choice. I am going to ensure
that the people of Scotland have that

00:07:32.600 --> 00:07:34.800
choice in this parliamentary session.

00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:47.030
Jackie Baillie: Before I begin my
questions, I put on record my condolences

00:07:47.030 --> 00:07:52.730
and those of the Scottish Labour Party on
the death of former MSP Cathie Craigie.

00:07:52.730 --> 00:07:54.650
Cathie was a much-loved colleague.

00:07:55.090 --> 00:07:58.110
She was a woman of great
determination and tenacity.

00:07:58.650 --> 00:08:01.590
She was the first woman to pass a
member’s bill in this Parliament,

00:08:02.090 --> 00:08:03.270
protecting mortgage holders.

00:08:03.710 --> 00:08:07.650
As the daughter of a miner, she
was a fierce campaigner for miners’

00:08:07.650 --> 00:08:08.810
welfare and wellbeing.

00:08:09.290 --> 00:08:13.410
She was committed to our Labour movement,
to the Parliament and to her constituents

00:08:13.410 --> 00:08:15.650
in Cumbernauld and Kilsyth.

00:08:15.650 --> 00:08:18.490
Our thoughts are with her
family at this difficult time.

00:08:18.890 --> 00:08:23.890
[Applause.]

00:08:25.310 --> 00:08:29.470
Presiding Officer, it is another
week, another court case.

00:08:29.470 --> 00:08:33.850
The Scottish Information Commissioner,
the man who is tasked with holding the

00:08:33.850 --> 00:08:38.250
Government to account, is challenging the
Scottish National Party in court over its

00:08:38.250 --> 00:08:40.490
refusal to release vital information.

00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:46.410
He says that the SNP Government risks
undermining the public’s right to access

00:08:46.410 --> 00:08:51.570
information and would relegate him to
little more than a “supervisory role”.

00:08:52.270 --> 00:08:56.970
The SNP Government stands accused
of seeking the power to decide what

00:08:56.970 --> 00:09:00.790
information the public can
see and what they cannot see.

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:06.430
The public’s right to know is on the
line, so I ask the First Minister what

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:12.890
is more important—the right of Scots to
know what their Government is up to or

00:09:12.890 --> 00:09:18.370
shielding himself, his London-based former
leader and his party from scrutiny?

00:09:22.830 --> 00:09:24.930
The First Minister: Before I come on
to the substance of Jackie Baillie’s

00:09:24.930 --> 00:09:29.350
question, although I made some remarks
on Tuesday in response to the Presiding

00:09:29.350 --> 00:09:33.770
Officer’s remarks, I associate myself
unreservedly with her comments about

00:09:33.770 --> 00:09:38.010
Cathie Craigie. Cathie Craigie was
a very distinguished member of the

00:09:38.010 --> 00:09:38.710
Scottish Parliament.

00:09:38.710 --> 00:09:42.770
She was a generous and kind lady, but
she was also a lady of good fun and

00:09:42.770 --> 00:09:46.870
good humour. We enjoyed her company, and
I pay tribute to her and associate myself

00:09:46.870 --> 00:09:52.250
entirely with Jackie Baillie’s words
of appreciation for Cathie Craigie.

00:09:52.250 --> 00:09:54.630
I express my condolences to her family.

00:09:54.630 --> 00:09:59.350
On the substantive issue
that Jackie Baillie raises,

00:10:00.250 --> 00:10:04.990
I am limited in what I can say, because
there are live court proceedings under way

00:10:04.990 --> 00:10:07.010
in which the Scottish
Government is a party.

00:10:07.730 --> 00:10:11.730
Jackie Baillie asked me what
I believe is important,

00:10:11.730 --> 00:10:14.310
and what I believe is
important is the rule of law.

00:10:15.090 --> 00:10:19.950
The Government believes that, in
the decisions that it is taking,

00:10:19.950 --> 00:10:24.170
it is operating fully in accordance with
the rule of law in relation to the freedom

00:10:24.170 --> 00:10:30.090
of information legislation and in
relation to the disclosure of information

00:10:30.090 --> 00:10:34.270
on which restrictions are
applied by court orders.

00:10:34.270 --> 00:10:39.470
I would not be fulfilling my duty
as First Minister if I did not

00:10:39.470 --> 00:10:43.330
ensure that the Government
was operating within the law.

00:10:43.330 --> 00:10:45.910
That is the basis of the judgment
that the Government has made,

00:10:46.130 --> 00:10:50.050
and, of course, that will be tested
in the courts in the action that is

00:10:50.050 --> 00:10:51.050
being pursued.

00:10:53.110 --> 00:10:57.550
Jackie Baillie: I thank the First Minister
for that response, but my difficulty is

00:10:57.550 --> 00:11:02.030
that I have seen how John Swinney
has operated over 27 years,

00:11:02.470 --> 00:11:06.510
going from honest John to the John
Swinney we now see before us,

00:11:06.770 --> 00:11:09.030
the architect of secret Scotland.

00:11:09.550 --> 00:11:14.490
This is the man who deleted WhatsApp
messages to hide from scrutiny over

00:11:14.490 --> 00:11:16.830
life-and-death decisions
during the pandemic.

00:11:17.350 --> 00:11:22.950
This is the man who spends the public’s
money going to court to deny the people of

00:11:22.950 --> 00:11:24.610
Scotland access to the truth.

00:11:24.910 --> 00:11:28.170
This is the man who appointed
a now-convicted fraudster,

00:11:28.550 --> 00:11:33.350
Peter Murrell, and this is the man
who oversaw a culture of cover-up,

00:11:33.350 --> 00:11:36.150
contaminating party and Government.

00:11:36.150 --> 00:11:38.830
Those are not isolated examples.

00:11:39.290 --> 00:11:44.210
They demonstrate a pattern of
behaviour—decades of cover-up and

00:11:44.210 --> 00:11:47.330
conspiracy with John
Swinney at its very heart.

00:11:47.630 --> 00:11:51.990
There is no failure too serious to
be covered up by this Government.

00:11:51.990 --> 00:11:57.910
Is it not time for John Swinney to emerge
from the shadows and finally put our

00:11:57.910 --> 00:12:00.070
country before his party?

00:12:07.250 --> 00:12:09.450
The First Minister: It is pretty obvious
that I have emerged from the shadows,

00:12:09.450 --> 00:12:14.670
because I won my own mandate in front
of the electorate of Scotland on 7 May.

00:12:20.410 --> 00:12:25.430
With the greatest of respect to
Jackie Baillie, because we have

00:12:25.430 --> 00:12:30.930
rubbed shoulders for 27 years in this

00:12:30.930 --> 00:12:35.730
institution—there are only the two
of us left from that first day who

00:12:39.110 --> 00:12:41.810
have been together continuously in this
Parliament over those 27 years—she has

00:12:42.230 --> 00:12:45.930
been able to observe my career in
government for longer than I had to

00:12:45.930 --> 00:12:49.950
observe her career in government,
if I can be so indelicate.

00:12:53.340 --> 00:12:57.240
If my memory serves me right, it was 12
months for Jackie Baillie in government

00:12:57.240 --> 00:13:03.000
and it is 19 years for me in government,
so we will just leave that judgment

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:04.380
for the public to make.

00:13:05.340 --> 00:13:10.380
My final point is this:
every single thing that

00:13:11.220 --> 00:13:15.780
Jackie Baillie said there, her
predecessor in that office,

00:13:16.260 --> 00:13:22.220
Anas Sarwar, said to me week after week
and month after month in the run-up to the

00:13:22.220 --> 00:13:26.840
election, and the Labour
Party went down to 17 seats.

00:13:26.840 --> 00:13:27.400
[Interruption.]

00:13:27.400 --> 00:13:33.180
As Mr Bibby shouts at me, having learned
nothing from the failure in the election

00:13:33.180 --> 00:13:37.400
and absolutely not having learned
that all that stuff does not work,

00:13:37.640 --> 00:13:40.900
the public can see the
Labour Party for what it is:

00:13:41.240 --> 00:13:45.900
a party with no ideas, no hope and no
direction—and that will not change at the

00:13:45.900 --> 00:13:46.900
weekend either.

00:13:53.800 --> 00:13:56.480
Jackie Baillie: What the First Minister
cannot escape is that everything that I

00:13:56.480 --> 00:14:01.960
said is all true. I have to say to him
to that, much as I have enjoyed these 27

00:14:01.960 --> 00:14:06.040
years together, I suspect that, if it
has anything to do with Stephen Flynn,

00:14:06.420 --> 00:14:08.240
I will outlast the First Minister—

00:14:08.640 --> 00:14:10.960
[Interruption.]

00:14:10.960 --> 00:14:12.780
The SNP would

00:14:14.660 --> 00:14:18.240
always rather hide a problem than fix it—

00:14:18.640 --> 00:14:20.470
[Interruption.]

00:14:20.470 --> 00:14:24.492
I am about to raise a very serious matter,
and I hope that members on the SNP benches

00:14:24.492 --> 00:14:25.370
will listen.

00:14:25.370 --> 00:14:26.576
The Presiding Officer: Yes. Let us
listen to the question, please.

00:14:26.576 --> 00:14:29.930
Jackie Baillie: The SNP would always
rather hide a problem than fix it,

00:14:30.310 --> 00:14:32.110
and that has real consequences.

00:14:32.810 --> 00:14:33.870
Let us look at one example.

00:14:34.410 --> 00:14:37.810
We have had years of promises
from John Swinney on ambulances.

00:14:38.390 --> 00:14:42.910
We challenged him on the crisis in
ambulance care in May last year,

00:14:43.410 --> 00:14:48.170
September last year, November last
year and again in January this year.

00:14:48.510 --> 00:14:54.210
He stood on that very spot and promised
to fix the problems in the Scottish

00:14:54.210 --> 00:14:56.770
Ambulance Service, but he never does.

00:14:57.510 --> 00:15:01.710
This summer, Julie Parker was
left dying on her bathroom floor,

00:15:01.710 --> 00:15:05.910
waiting for help. The police
phoned 999 when they found her.

00:15:06.410 --> 00:15:08.610
They phoned again when no ambulance came.

00:15:09.130 --> 00:15:13.770
It took four hours for an ambulance to
arrive, but by then it was too late.

00:15:14.310 --> 00:15:16.330
She was just 55 years old.

00:15:16.870 --> 00:15:20.410
That is the human cost of SNP failure.

00:15:21.130 --> 00:15:25.090
When will John Swinney realise that
the Government’s dishonesty and broken

00:15:25.090 --> 00:15:28.150
promises have consequences?

00:15:33.270 --> 00:15:38.910
The First Minister: Jackie Baillie
uses very strong language, and

00:15:38.910 --> 00:15:42.830
I will treat the substance of the case
as I should do as First Minister.

00:15:43.770 --> 00:15:47.970
Before I get to that, however, I note what
she said about freedom of information

00:15:48.630 --> 00:15:50.730
issues and transparency in the Government.

00:15:52.570 --> 00:15:58.410
In the past six months, the
Government has received 3,218 freedom

00:15:58.410 --> 00:16:03.930
of information requests; that is a 50
per cent increase on five years ago.

00:16:04.630 --> 00:16:09.110
The Government has responded to 96
per cent of those requests within the

00:16:09.110 --> 00:16:14.890
statutory timescale, so I refute,
with evidence, the argument

00:16:14.890 --> 00:16:18.030
that Jackie Baillie puts forward
that the Government is not

00:16:18.030 --> 00:16:19.750
fulfilling its obligations.

00:16:20.370 --> 00:16:26.290
I will now move on to the very serious
issue of the human cost of the case

00:16:26.290 --> 00:16:27.530
involving Julie Parker.

00:16:27.790 --> 00:16:31.450
I am familiar with the case, and the
Cabinet Secretary for Health and Care has

00:16:31.450 --> 00:16:35.770
offered to meet Julie Parker’s family
following the outcome of a review that is

00:16:35.770 --> 00:16:39.650
currently being undertaken by the
Scottish Ambulance Service and NHS

00:16:39.650 --> 00:16:45.550
Ayrshire and Arran. Cases such as Julie
Parker’s, and the case of Elizabeth

00:16:45.550 --> 00:16:48.310
Crammond, which Jackie Baillie
raised with me last week,

00:16:48.710 --> 00:16:54.330
are examples of unacceptable health
service performance and I make

00:16:54.330 --> 00:16:56.430
no attempt to shield from that.

00:16:56.850 --> 00:17:01.290
We work hard to improve outcomes, but
unfortunately there will be cases in which

00:17:01.290 --> 00:17:04.110
the outcomes are not what
we would want them to be,

00:17:04.110 --> 00:17:07.070
and we have to learn the lessons
and focus on improvements.

00:17:07.630 --> 00:17:10.150
That is what drives the
Government’s agenda.

00:17:11.310 --> 00:17:15.370
We try to ensure that we deliver
the improvements that are necessary,

00:17:15.370 --> 00:17:19.710
and the health secretary will engage
with Julie Parker’s family to

00:17:19.710 --> 00:17:21.310
discuss the issue.

00:17:21.310 --> 00:17:24.970
I will close by expressing my
condolences to Julie Parker’s family.

00:17:25.330 --> 00:17:28.350
I read this morning, in
the Daily Record newspaper,

00:17:28.350 --> 00:17:33.850
the transcript of the call between
police officers and the Scottish

00:17:33.850 --> 00:17:39.490
Ambulance Service. It is profoundly
disturbing and requires to be examined,

00:17:39.490 --> 00:17:40.910
and that is exactly what will be done.

00:17:43.670 --> 00:17:46.990
Ross Greer: I pass on the Scottish Greens’
condolences to Cathie Craigie’s family and

00:17:46.990 --> 00:17:48.410
her friends in the Labour Party.

00:17:48.930 --> 00:17:53.590
Her work in establishing a register of
landlords in Scotland started an important

00:17:53.590 --> 00:17:56.870
journey, which the Parliament is still on,
to protect those in this country who rent

00:17:56.870 --> 00:18:00.110
their home, and it has made a real
difference to people across the country.

00:18:01.310 --> 00:18:05.810
I start by congratulating the community
groups across the country whose campaign

00:18:05.810 --> 00:18:09.410
against hyperscale data centres has
forced the Scottish Government to act.

00:18:09.870 --> 00:18:12.630
There is still some confusion,
however, over what the Government’s

00:18:12.630 --> 00:18:16.390
position now is. Ministers say that
they will produce new guidance for

00:18:16.390 --> 00:18:17.490
councils by Christmas.

00:18:17.490 --> 00:18:19.430
That is welcome—it is what
the Greens called for.

00:18:19.430 --> 00:18:22.890
However, ministers have also said that
no decisions will be taken before then.

00:18:23.490 --> 00:18:26.530
I struggle to reconcile that with
the reality of the timeline.

00:18:27.030 --> 00:18:30.370
The proposals for data centres for
Larbert, Greenock and Auchtertool could

00:18:30.370 --> 00:18:31.810
all be decided before then.

00:18:32.110 --> 00:18:36.050
The deadline for determination on
the Larbert application is 4 October,

00:18:36.050 --> 00:18:40.050
and the developers in Greenock are
working towards a deadline in November.

00:18:40.050 --> 00:18:44.310
The Scottish Government has said
categorically that no decisions should be

00:18:44.310 --> 00:18:46.610
made before the guidance
is produced, at Christmas.

00:18:46.610 --> 00:18:51.830
Has it, therefore, instructed Inverclyde,
Falkirk and Fife councils to pause their

00:18:51.830 --> 00:18:53.810
determination of those live applications?

00:18:57.560 --> 00:19:01.280
The First Minister: We do not anticipate
that any applications will be determined

00:19:01.280 --> 00:19:04.840
by planning authorities before our
planning guidance is issued later

00:19:04.840 --> 00:19:05.840
this year.

00:19:08.800 --> 00:19:14.780
Ross Greer: I struggle to reconcile
what ministers said yesterday

00:19:14.780 --> 00:19:17.700
with what they voted for and what the
First Minister has just said today.

00:19:18.120 --> 00:19:21.540
I and the Greens, and communities across
the country who were celebrating last

00:19:21.540 --> 00:19:25.260
night because they thought that there
was a pause—a formal pause—are trying to

00:19:25.260 --> 00:19:26.980
accept the Government’s
position in good faith.

00:19:27.460 --> 00:19:31.300
However, MSPs across this Parliament left
the chamber last night confused because

00:19:31.300 --> 00:19:34.480
they could not reconcile what ministers
said with what they voted for.

00:19:34.480 --> 00:19:37.640
The Minister for Business and Fair Work
said that no decisions are expected before

00:19:37.640 --> 00:19:41.080
Christmas, based on discussion with
council officials—that is similar to what

00:19:41.080 --> 00:19:42.280
the First Minister has just said.

00:19:42.280 --> 00:19:44.820
However, there is nothing in the
public domain to confirm that.

00:19:44.820 --> 00:19:49.700
Today, the Falkirk Council website still
has the deadline for the determination of

00:19:49.700 --> 00:19:52.300
the Larbert application as 4 October.

00:19:52.880 --> 00:19:57.420
Is the First Minister saying that, if a
decision on any hyperscale data centre is

00:19:57.420 --> 00:20:01.340
made before Christmas, which some councils
are still on the record as intending to

00:20:01.340 --> 00:20:05.840
do, the Government will step in, stop
that and make the final decision itself,

00:20:05.840 --> 00:20:07.720
based on the new guidance
that it is producing?

00:20:09.280 --> 00:20:14.680
The First Minister: As I said in my first
answer, we do not anticipate that any

00:20:14.680 --> 00:20:18.380
applications will be determined by
planning authorities before the planning

00:20:18.380 --> 00:20:19.980
guidance is issued, later this year.

00:20:20.540 --> 00:20:23.340
Through the notification direction
that we issued during the summer,

00:20:23.800 --> 00:20:27.100
we are closely monitoring live
applications and their intended timescales

00:20:27.100 --> 00:20:30.500
for determination, hence the statement
that I just made to Parliament.

00:20:30.500 --> 00:20:34.640
Although Scottish ministers have
call-in powers, we use them sparingly,

00:20:34.640 --> 00:20:38.260
recognising and respecting the important
role of local authorities in making

00:20:38.260 --> 00:20:39.560
decisions in their areas.

00:20:40.080 --> 00:20:44.500
We can, however, take steps to call in any
application where that is necessary and

00:20:44.500 --> 00:20:46.680
deemed to be a matter
of national importance.

00:20:47.360 --> 00:20:51.520
Ministers can also recall any planning
appeal rather than have the decision made

00:20:51.520 --> 00:20:55.060
by the Scottish Government reporter,
and we have used that power recently in

00:20:55.060 --> 00:20:58.000
relation to the live planning appeal
for a data centre in Edinburgh.

00:20:58.440 --> 00:21:00.900
I hope that that provides the
clarity that Ross Greer is seeking.

00:21:03.000 --> 00:21:06.140
Ross Greer: The First Minister’s
answer does not provide clarity.

00:21:06.140 --> 00:21:08.960
It hints at something—it strongly hints
that the Government would be willing to

00:21:08.960 --> 00:21:11.600
recall those decisions—and I
welcome that in good faith.

00:21:11.600 --> 00:21:15.980
However, I say again that Falkirk Council
still states that the determination

00:21:15.980 --> 00:21:17.760
deadline is 4 October.

00:21:18.100 --> 00:21:21.720
The Government’s private discussions
with local councils appear to be quite

00:21:21.720 --> 00:21:24.280
different from what is
currently on the public record.

00:21:24.280 --> 00:21:27.060
That needs to be reconciled if our
communities are to have confidence

00:21:27.060 --> 00:21:28.060
in the process.

00:21:28.800 --> 00:21:32.860
It is welcome that we have President
Connolly of Ireland here in Scotland this

00:21:32.860 --> 00:21:36.300
week, not just for her leadership on
solidarity with the people of Palestine,

00:21:36.300 --> 00:21:40.340
but because Ireland is a stark example
of what happens when greedy technology

00:21:40.340 --> 00:21:42.700
companies are allowed to
build whatever they want.

00:21:43.080 --> 00:21:46.980
A third of Ireland’s entire domestic
energy supply goes to powering data

00:21:46.980 --> 00:21:50.920
centres, but that pales in comparison
with what is proposed for Scotland.

00:21:50.920 --> 00:21:55.740
The first 24 applications here would
require one and a half times our entire

00:21:55.740 --> 00:21:57.280
national energy demand.

00:21:57.280 --> 00:22:00.140
Our Parliament was warned, earlier
this week, that that could push up

00:22:00.140 --> 00:22:03.020
energy bills. That is exactly
what has happened in Ireland.

00:22:05.320 --> 00:22:07.180
Because of data centres, the energy bills
of consumers and households in Ireland

00:22:07.180 --> 00:22:09.800
have gone up by the
equivalent of £300 a year.

00:22:10.300 --> 00:22:13.760
Councils cannot take that issue into
account when making piecemeal decisions on

00:22:13.760 --> 00:22:14.720
individual applications.

00:22:14.720 --> 00:22:18.220
Therefore, will the guidance that the
First Minister’s Government is producing

00:22:18.220 --> 00:22:24.040
include a clear national strategy on
how much of our electricity supply can

00:22:24.040 --> 00:22:28.180
be used by hyperscale data centres before
they damage our climate and push up our

00:22:28.180 --> 00:22:29.180
energy bills?

00:22:32.770 --> 00:22:37.490
The First Minister: The national guidance
will set out all the relevant issues that

00:22:37.490 --> 00:22:40.770
have to be taken into account in
the determination of applications.

00:22:41.850 --> 00:22:43.550
To reiterate one of the
points that I made earlier,

00:22:43.550 --> 00:22:46.310
I state that, through the notification
direction that we issued during the

00:22:46.310 --> 00:22:50.770
summer, we are closely monitoring live
applications and the intended timescales

00:22:50.770 --> 00:22:51.770
for their determination.

00:22:52.510 --> 00:22:56.030
Mr Greer raised the
role of tech companies.

00:22:56.950 --> 00:23:01.730
The Government insisted, in
its amendment yesterday,

00:23:01.730 --> 00:23:06.230
that there has to be regulation in
the space around tech companies.

00:23:06.230 --> 00:23:10.130
That was a central part of the
Government’s amendment to the Green motion

00:23:10.130 --> 00:23:13.250
last night. I am pleased that the
Government’s amendment was carried,

00:23:13.690 --> 00:23:17.490
because it strengthens the representations
that my Government can make to the United

00:23:17.490 --> 00:23:22.730
Kingdom Government to ensure that we are
pressing for the appropriate regulatory

00:23:22.730 --> 00:23:25.630
environment, given the concerns that
have been expressed over the weekend,

00:23:26.190 --> 00:23:28.350
particularly about tech companies.

00:23:28.870 --> 00:23:33.630
I was a bit surprised that the Greens
abstained on that particular vote at the

00:23:33.630 --> 00:23:36.630
end of the debate, because
that regulation is necessary.

00:23:36.870 --> 00:23:41.450
It is at the centre of the concerns
that my Cabinet is wrestling with about

00:23:41.450 --> 00:23:44.690
violence against women and girls, as,
because of the online environment,

00:23:44.690 --> 00:23:46.370
there is a greater need for regulation.

00:23:47.650 --> 00:23:52.150
As we make progress on some of those
questions, there are big issues for us to

00:23:52.150 --> 00:23:56.470
wrestle with, and I hope that the Greens
will be partners with us in that respect.

00:23:56.470 --> 00:24:01.530
As Mr Greer made reference to the
presence of President Connolly,

00:24:01.530 --> 00:24:05.950
I note that it was an absolute pleasure
for my wife and I to welcome President

00:24:05.950 --> 00:24:09.430
Connolly and her husband
to Scotland yesterday.

00:24:10.250 --> 00:24:14.630
President Connolly represents
outstanding leadership in the world.

00:24:14.910 --> 00:24:19.970
She is a voice for peace,
for dialogue and for respect.

00:24:20.550 --> 00:24:25.530
She has much to say to Scotland, and
Scotland is listening very carefully to

00:24:25.530 --> 00:24:27.110
what she has to say.

00:24:30.950 --> 00:24:36.950
Russell Findlay: I, too, express my
condolences and those of my party to the

00:24:36.950 --> 00:24:38.650
family of Cathie Craigie.

00:24:39.290 --> 00:24:44.677
John Swinney’s new friends in Sinn Féin
have never denounced Irish Republican

00:24:44.677 --> 00:24:50.330
Army terrorism. They still
justify the massacre of innocent

00:24:50.330 --> 00:24:54.610
men, women and children,
including Scottish soldiers.

00:24:55.270 --> 00:25:00.650
Mr Swinney shamefully told IRA
victims and their grieving families to

00:25:00.650 --> 00:25:06.310
“move on”, but the same
politician has never

00:25:06.310 --> 00:25:11.890
accepted the decisive result of
Scotland’s independence referendum,

00:25:11.890 --> 00:25:17.090
and John Swinney is now working with
Sinn Féin to break up our country.

00:25:17.850 --> 00:25:23.510
Why should those maimed or
bereaved by the IRA move on

00:25:23.510 --> 00:25:27.790
when the Scottish National
Party refuses to move on?

00:25:31.270 --> 00:25:36.250
The First Minister: I have always
expressed my sympathy to the

00:25:36.250 --> 00:25:37.470
victims of terrorism.

00:25:38.170 --> 00:25:43.550
Indeed, just last month, I
represented Scotland at the first

00:25:43.550 --> 00:25:47.570
national day of remembrance
for the victims of terrorism,

00:25:48.030 --> 00:25:51.310
which was convened by the
Prime Minister in London.

00:25:52.230 --> 00:25:56.330
On that occasion, I met families
of the victims of terrorism,

00:25:56.570 --> 00:26:01.210
many of whom were affected by
the Lockerbie bombing in 1988.

00:26:01.730 --> 00:26:06.590
I unreservedly condemn
terrorism and any terrorist act,

00:26:06.590 --> 00:26:07.590
and I always have done.

00:26:08.330 --> 00:26:14.110
I also accepted, in good grace,
the outcome of the referendum

00:26:14.110 --> 00:26:15.390
in 2014.

00:26:15.790 --> 00:26:20.790
[Interruption.]

00:26:23.570 --> 00:26:28.550
In the aftermath of that
referendum, I also participated,

00:26:28.550 --> 00:26:31.070
in good faith, in the work
of the Smith commission,

00:26:31.650 --> 00:26:35.950
which reviewed the powers of the
Parliament and led to significant

00:26:35.950 --> 00:26:37.550
increases in its powers.

00:26:40.950 --> 00:26:44.470
I put forward arguments in that process
to ensure that this Parliament could make

00:26:44.470 --> 00:26:47.150
more impact on the lives
of people in Scotland.

00:26:48.270 --> 00:26:51.730
I did that, in good faith, to strengthen
the powers of the Parliament within the

00:26:51.730 --> 00:26:55.450
United Kingdom. Mr Findlay’s
point is completely fatuous,

00:26:55.910 --> 00:26:56.910
as usual.

00:26:58.590 --> 00:27:03.210
People in this country would be surprised
if I did not stick to the principled

00:27:03.210 --> 00:27:05.190
reason why I entered politics.

00:27:05.790 --> 00:27:10.110
I entered politics because I became
convinced that the people who are best

00:27:10.110 --> 00:27:15.730
placed to decide the future of our country
are the people who choose to live here in

00:27:15.730 --> 00:27:19.730
Scotland, which is why we need the
ability to make a decision—a choice—about

00:27:19.730 --> 00:27:23.670
Scotland’s future in a referendum,
and I will deliver that in this

00:27:23.670 --> 00:27:24.670
parliamentary term.

00:27:31.740 --> 00:27:36.060
Russell Findlay: We know that John Swinney
has denounced terrorism, but has he asked

00:27:36.060 --> 00:27:39.200
his Sinn Féin friends
to denounce terrorism?

00:27:40.020 --> 00:27:45.940
We just heard, yet again, that John
Swinney only cares about one thing:

00:27:46.880 --> 00:27:51.640
making Scotland poorer and weaker
by leaving the United Kingdom.

00:27:51.960 --> 00:27:57.360
He and his party have never respected the
outcome of the independence referendum,

00:27:57.700 --> 00:28:03.058
and Scotland is paying a heavy price for
the SNP’s obsession—a broken national

00:28:03.058 --> 00:28:07.500
health service, failing schools,
victims betrayed by the justice system,

00:28:07.900 --> 00:28:12.780
record high taxes and funding
out-of-control state benefits.

00:28:13.420 --> 00:28:19.340
The people of Scotland want the Government
to focus on the issues that matter to

00:28:19.340 --> 00:28:24.980
them—those issues, not
independence—so why is his

00:28:24.980 --> 00:28:30.140
constitutional obsession
more important than fixing

00:28:30.140 --> 00:28:32.840
Scotland’s broken public services?

00:28:34.580 --> 00:28:39.420
The First Minister: It is really
interesting that, today, Russell Findlay

00:28:39.420 --> 00:28:42.880
has pursued the same line of
questioning as Malcolm Offord.

00:28:42.880 --> 00:28:48.760
That displays to us that the merger
is under way between those two

00:28:48.760 --> 00:28:53.200
forces on that side of the
chamber and, my goodness,

00:28:53.740 --> 00:28:59.660
it will be some marriage of
convenience over there between

00:28:59.660 --> 00:29:04.680
the Conservative Party and Reform, with
some of the stuff they all come out with.

00:29:04.920 --> 00:29:06.420
That will be a sight worth seeing.

00:29:07.600 --> 00:29:13.540
They have a lot in common, because
Malcolm Offord was a member of the

00:29:13.540 --> 00:29:17.360
Government that inflicted
austerity on this country—

00:29:17.360 --> 00:29:17.613
[Interruption.]

00:29:17.613 --> 00:29:17.992
Stephen Kerr: Answer the question.

00:29:17.992 --> 00:29:19.320
The Presiding Officer: Mr Kerr,
let us hear the First Minister.

00:29:21.400 --> 00:29:26.320
The First Minister: Malcolm Offord was a
member and Russell Findlay was a supporter

00:29:26.320 --> 00:29:31.300
of a Government that inflicted
austerity on this country.

00:29:31.300 --> 00:29:35.260
They represent political
traditions that wanted Brexit,

00:29:35.680 --> 00:29:39.340
which has undeniably made
us poorer as a consequence.

00:29:39.340 --> 00:29:44.600
Therefore, I will take no lessons
from Russell Findlay or his chum

00:29:44.600 --> 00:29:45.560
Malcolm Offord—

00:29:45.560 --> 00:29:45.800
Stephen Kerr: Answer the question!

00:29:45.800 --> 00:29:45.880
The Presiding Officer:
Excuse me, First Minister.

00:29:45.880 --> 00:29:49.580
Mr Kerr, as I have already said,
please stop shouting from the back.

00:29:49.580 --> 00:29:52.460
You can express a view without
yelling at the top of your voice.

00:29:52.460 --> 00:29:53.460
We are trying to hear the First Minister.

00:29:53.880 --> 00:29:56.140
If I cannot hear him from here, how
is anyone else meant to hear him?

00:29:56.480 --> 00:29:57.600
First Minister, please conclude.

00:29:57.600 --> 00:30:01.580
The First Minister: I will take no
lessons from the new chums in the Scottish

00:30:01.580 --> 00:30:06.840
Parliament, Russell Findlay and Malcolm
Offord—the people who gave us decline,

00:30:07.340 --> 00:30:08.720
austerity and Brexit.

00:30:08.720 --> 00:30:11.940
Instead, I will inspire people
with a positive vision of

00:30:11.940 --> 00:30:13.180
independence for Scotland.

00:30:21.020 --> 00:30:25.160
Alex Cole-Hamilton: As other members
have done, I, too, extend the heartfelt

00:30:25.160 --> 00:30:28.240
condolences of the Scottish Liberal
Democrats to the Labour Party,

00:30:28.240 --> 00:30:31.420
the family of Cathie Craigie
and everybody who loved her.

00:30:31.420 --> 00:30:35.020
She was a towering force in our
politics and she will be much missed.

00:30:36.040 --> 00:30:41.260
This week, Lady Justice Thirlwall
published her report into the horrific

00:30:41.260 --> 00:30:45.160
murder of babies at the
Countess of Chester hospital.

00:30:45.160 --> 00:30:50.280
That review was instructed following
the conviction of neonatal nurse

00:30:50.280 --> 00:30:53.160
Lucy Letby. That was a shocking case.

00:30:53.160 --> 00:30:56.580
What has the First Minister’s
Government learned from

00:31:00.600 --> 00:31:03.240
the inquiry?

00:31:03.240 --> 00:31:07.000
The First Minister: Obviously, it is
important for us all to learn lessons from

00:31:07.000 --> 00:31:11.400
the inquiry, with regard to ensuring
the quality and safety of patient care.

00:31:12.120 --> 00:31:17.800
As Mr Cole-Hamilton will know, an ongoing,
persistent priority of the Scottish

00:31:17.800 --> 00:31:23.680
Government is to ensure that we strengthen
patient safety in every respect of our

00:31:23.680 --> 00:31:26.040
health service care and treatment.

00:31:26.360 --> 00:31:29.280
That lies at the heart of the answer to
the question from Jackie Baillie that I

00:31:29.280 --> 00:31:34.360
responded to, because vital issues
of patient safety are involved in

00:31:34.360 --> 00:31:38.920
that respect. That will be the focus
of our interrogation of that report,

00:31:38.920 --> 00:31:41.575
to ensure that any issues that
are relevant to Scotland,

00:31:41.575 --> 00:31:46.600
and particularly to maternity services,
are learned about and applied.

00:31:49.700 --> 00:31:52.440
Alex Cole-Hamilton: I am glad to hear
that but, although the inquiry’s

00:31:52.440 --> 00:31:56.260
recommendations stop at the border,
the risk to babies does not.

00:31:56.780 --> 00:32:02.160
Parents will want to know that newborns
here are given the same protection as

00:32:02.160 --> 00:32:03.240
babies everywhere else.

00:32:03.800 --> 00:32:08.660
Lady Justice Thirlwall’s recommendations
include in-cot cameras with live streaming

00:32:08.660 --> 00:32:14.540
video; tighter controls on access to
insulin; and a duty on every health

00:32:14.540 --> 00:32:19.400
trust to monitor all baby deaths
and escalate concerning patterns.

00:32:19.400 --> 00:32:24.700
It now falls to this Government to choose
whether to adopt those vital protections,

00:32:24.700 --> 00:32:30.380
because—God forbid—should Scotland
ever have its own Lucy Letby,

00:32:30.380 --> 00:32:35.020
we must have every possible safeguard
in place to keep our babies safe.

00:32:36.440 --> 00:32:40.980
Will the First Minister’s Government
include equivalent protections in

00:32:40.980 --> 00:32:42.520
Scotland—and by when?

00:32:44.060 --> 00:32:49.820
The First Minister: Mr Cole-Hamilton
raises important issues, and

00:32:49.820 --> 00:32:55.240
I expect them to be central to the work
that the Government is taking forward

00:32:55.240 --> 00:33:00.940
through the maternity and neonatal task
force, which was set up to promote

00:33:00.940 --> 00:33:05.740
improvements in maternity and neonatal
care, and pregnancy and birth outcomes.

00:33:06.440 --> 00:33:12.340
We have established the
task force to undertake

00:33:12.340 --> 00:33:16.720
that work. It would make logical and
rational sense for the task force to look

00:33:16.720 --> 00:33:22.620
at the issues that arise out of the report
to which Mr Cole-Hamilton refers and

00:33:22.620 --> 00:33:25.320
to identify, based on existing
practice in Scotland,

00:33:25.960 --> 00:33:27.000
where there should be strengthening.

00:33:27.220 --> 00:33:30.960
I encourage the task force to
undertake the work in that way,

00:33:30.960 --> 00:33:36.060
to make sure that the lessons that
can be applied are applied to

00:33:36.060 --> 00:33:40.300
meet the high standards that Mr
Cole-Hamilton puts to me today.

00:33:41.500 --> 00:33:42.820
The Presiding Officer:
Thank you, First Minister.

00:33:43.840 --> 00:33:46.000
When I referred to Mr Kerr earlier,
I should have said that I meant

00:33:46.000 --> 00:33:49.920
Stephen Kerr. There are, of course,
five Mr Kerrs in the chamber,

00:33:49.920 --> 00:33:52.120
and I apologise to the other four.
